  "status": "Refused",
  "description": "1. Introduction  \nThis application seeks to amend Condition 3 of planning permission 2024/0356, which currently restricts the building’s use to Class E(g) (offices). Given that consent for Class E has already been granted, we respectfully request an amendment to this condition to permit the full spectrum of uses within Class E (a to g), as delineated in the Town and Country Planning (Use Classes) Order 1987 (as amended).\n2. Site and Context  \nThe property at 69–71 Great Eastern Street is a multi-storey period structure in the Shoreditch Priority Office Area. A dynamic blend of office, retail, hospitality, and creative enterprises surrounds it. The building, which previously functioned as an educational facility and has now received approval for Class E(g), features multiple levels with independent access points. Notably, the ground floor includes a dedicated street-facing entrance (double doors to the west), making it ideally suited for retail, food, or service operations without impacting the functionality of the upper floors.\n3. Planning Policy Alignment  \nLP26 – Economic Development: The flexibility allowed by Class E supports a diverse and adaptable economy, consistent with Hackney’s growth strategy and post-pandemic resilience objectives.  \nLP27—Offices: The potential for office use is preserved while complementary ground-floor activities that enhance the office ecosystem are permitted.  \nLP28 – Affordable Workspace: Broader usage encourages start-ups and service-oriented businesses, fostering street-level activity and inclusive economic development.\n4. Precedent and Character  \nThe proposal is consistent with established patterns in Shoreditch, where numerous comparable buildings operate under flexible Class E arrangements. This model has bolstered local vibrancy and commercial sustainability. Mixed-use developments—such as retail or café operations on the ground floor alongside creative and office spaces above—have effectively increased foot traffic, enhanced safety, and adapted heritage buildings to contemporary needs. The current proposal echoes this strategy and supports Hackney’s broader regeneration and placemaking aims.\n5. Access and Amenities  \nThe ground-floor unit possesses dedicated access, ensuring that upper-level activities remain undisturbed. Existing vertical circulation facilitates a diverse range of tenancies without conflict. Additionally, servicing, waste management, and noise control comply with existing regulations.\n6. Conclusion  \nThe proposed amendment enables the building to better align with market demand while preserving its commercial character. Granting full Class E usage will:  \n- Promote long-term adaptability  \n- Enhance street-level vibrancy and foot traffic  \n- Align with Hackney’s strategic economic policies  \nSo, we would like to ask for the variation of Condition 3 to allow the comprehensive range of Class E uses across the site while maintaining adherence to all other approved conditions and plans.",
  "decision_conditions": [
    "&lt;p&gt;The proposed development would introduce changes that would materially alter the development from that of the originally approved permission. As a result this proposal goes beyond the scope of Section 73 of the Town and Country Planning Act 1990 and is contrary to guidance within the National Planning Practice Guidance, specifically paragraph 001 Reference ID: 17a-001-20140306.&lt;/p&gt;"
  ],
